result.llm_view:
Based on my analysis of the nanort codebase, I'll implement the GetBoundingBoxOfTriangle function. The function needs to compute the axis-aligned bounding box of a triangle given vertices and face indices.

Looking at the codebase structure, I can see that:
1. Vertices are stored as T* (float/double array)
2. Faces contain indices to vertex positions
3. Each triangle is defined by 3 consecutive vertex indices in the faces array
4. The function should find the min/max coordinates of the three vertices of the triangle

Here's my implementation:

```cpp
    const unsigned int *face = &faces[index * 3];

    const T *v0 = &vertices[face[0] * 3];
    const T *v1 = &vertices[face[1] * 3];
    const T *v2 = &vertices[face[2] * 3];

    // Initialize bounding box with first vertex
    T min_x = v0[0];
    T min_y = v0[1];
    T min_z = v0[2];
    T max_x = v0[0];
    T max_y = v0[1];
    T max_z = v0[2];

    // Compare with second vertex
    min_x = std::min(min_x, std::min(v1[0], v2[0]));
    min_y = std::min(min_y, std::min(v1[1], v2[1]));
    min_z = std::min(min_z, std::min(v1[2], v2[2]));
    max_x = std::max(max_x, std::max(v1[0], v2[0]));
    max_y = std::max(max_y, std::max(v1[1], v2[1]));
    max_z = std::max(max_z, std::max(v1[2], v2[2]));

    // Set the bounding box
    (*bmin)[0] = min_x;
    (*bmin)[1] = min_y;
    (*bmin)[2] = min_z;
    (*bmax)[0] = max_x;
    (*bmax)[1] = max_y;
    (*bmax)[2] = max_z;
```

